  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 243 PM CDT Thu Aug 6 2026\n\nThis afternoon through Friday: A dramatic increase in moisture\ntook place today allowing for a few pulse type storms with\ndownbursts over roughly the southern half of the area. This\nactivity will diminish with sunset, then expect quiet weather\nconditions overnight. Friday should see temperatures/heat stress\nremain elevated/limited as advertised, and no changes have been \nmade to the previous messaging. In terms of convective potential \nFriday, anticyclonic flow will be a little more prevalent and this\nwill limit storm coverage, but there are some signals in the REFS\nshowing greater potential for an organized convective system to \nour north to potentially affect far northern portions of the area,\nincluding the Highway 82 corridor. In southeast portions of the \narea including the lower Pine Belt, some influence from the \nseabreeze front could increase diurnal convective rainfall chances\nthere. /EC/ \n\nThis weekend through mid next week (Saturday through next \nWednesday): The synoptic pattern will shift to a deepening mean \nridge over the Four Corners while the southeast states caught in \nbetween ridge to the west and subtropical ridge off the Atlantic \nseaboard. Surface boundary layer moisture will increase/higher \nsurface dewpoints this weekend (72F to 77F). Combined with \nseasonable warmth (highs 93F to 96F), heat stress will only spread\nand increase in area, continuing into early next week. HWO \ngraphic looks good for the weekend and continued the threat into \nearly next week (Tuesday). Further refinements and heat headlines \nwill likely be needed.\n\nLow level moist advection and increased deep moisture aloft will \nincrease this the weekend (PWs in the 1.7 to 2.1 inch range late \nweekend into next week). Rain chances will mainly be summertime \nvariety (diurnally induced) Thursday south of Interstate 20 and \nalong and southeast of Natchez Trace into the weekend and \nspreading northwest into early next week. Coverage will be \nisolated to at times scattered coverage (20 to 40 percent range) \nwith highest coverage in the Pine Belt, with potential sea breeze \ninfluence. This pattern does not favor noteworthy organized storm \nthreat, but scattered stronger gusty storms with locally heavy \nrain cannot be ruled out, especially late weekend through mid next\nweek. /DC/EC/",
